    "field": "Coupled atmosphere-ocean energy budget",
    "problem": "Coupled climate models exhibit serious biases when simulating El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) energy pathways, underestimating key aspects like ocean heat content variability and misrepresenting top-of-atmosphere radiation responses, which this paper evaluates against observational data to identify and quantify these shortcomings.",
    "method": "This paper applies a vertically integrated energy budget framework to quantify ENSO-driven energy perturbations in observational datasets, using these results as a benchmark to evaluate systematic biases in the energy pathways, ocean heat content, and radiative responses simulated by CMIP climate models.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify ENSO-driven energy budget perturbations using observational datasets and use these results as a benchmark to evaluate how well coupled climate models from CMIP3 and CMIP5 archives simulate these energy pathways.",
